In Odoo, a Reordering Rule defines when and how much of a product should be automatically replenished. The concept of reordering rules is to automate the procurement of products, either by purchase, manufacturing, or transfer, when certain conditions are met.

Here are the primary attributes or types of information you define in a Reordering Rule:

    Product: The product for which the rule is being defined.

    Warehouse: The warehouse where the product is or will be stored.

    Minimum Quantity: This is the minimum product quantity that should trigger the replenishment. When the quantity on hand goes below this minimum quantity, Odoo will create a procurement to reach the maximum quantity.

    Maximum Quantity: This is the quantity to which the product should be replenished. For example, if you have a minimum quantity of 5 units and a maximum quantity of 10 units, when the stock goes below 5, Odoo will create a procurement for the quantity that will bring the stock level back to 10.

    Quantity Multiple: If set, the order/production quantity will be rounded up to this multiple. For instance, if you want to order batteries in packs of 10, you'll set the quantity multiple to 10. So, if your stock goes below the minimum and the system calculates that 15 units are required, it will round up to 20 (next multiple of 10).

    Lead Days: This determines how many days before the expected stock-out the procurement should be created and validated. It helps in considering the expected delivery or production time.

    Company: If operating a multi-company environment, you can specify which company this rule applies to.

    Route: You can define the route for this product (e.g., Buy, Manufacture, Replenish). This tells Odoo how to handle the procurement - whether to buy the product, produce it, or transfer it from another location.

These are the primary components of a Reordering Rule in Odoo. The system monitors stock levels, and when the quantity of a product drops below the defined minimum, a procurement is automatically triggered to bring the stock level back up to the defined maximum. This ensures a smoother inventory management process and helps in reducing stockouts.

In Odoo, "MTO" stands for "Make to Order." It is a route for a product which dictates how the product should be procured or produced once a sales order for the product is confirmed. Here's a brief overview:
Make to Order (MTO)

When a product is configured with the "Make to Order" route:

    Upon confirmation of a sales order, a procurement is generated for that product.
    Depending on the product's configuration, this procurement can lead to either the creation of a purchase order (to buy from a vendor) or a manufacturing order (to produce in-house).

The MTO route ensures that products are not produced or purchased until an order for them is confirmed. This is in contrast to the traditional stock-based approach where items are produced or purchased in advance and then taken from stock when sold.
Other Routes in Odoo:

    Buy: This route allows for the product to be purchased from vendors. When the stock goes below the minimum stock rules or when triggered by an MTO route, a purchase order is generated.

    Manufacture: This route indicates that the product is manufactured in-house. When stock rules are triggered or when coupled with MTO, it generates a manufacturing order.

    Dropship: This is another route where, upon confirmation of a sales order, a purchase order is generated to buy the product directly from the vendor and have it shipped directly to the customer. The product never comes into your own stock.

    Replenish on Order (MTO + Buy): This combines the MTO route with the Buy route. When a sales order is confirmed, a purchase order is generated to buy the product from the vendor.

    Manufacture on Order (MTO + Manufacture): Combines the MTO route with the Manufacture route. When a sales order is confirmed, a manufacturing order is generated to produce the product.

When setting up routes, it's important to consider the specific business needs and configure products accordingly. Routes determine the flow of products through the supply chain and have significant implications for inventory management, lead times, and costs.
In Odoo, the concept of routes defines the path that products will follow throughout a warehouse. They are particularly useful to configure complex paths such as drop-shipping, cross-docking, and multi-warehousing scenarios. Here are the main types of routes and their typical use cases:

    Buy:
        Used when products are bought from a supplier.
        Typically results in a Purchase Order being created when the product's stock goes below the re-ordering rule threshold.

    Manufacture:
        Used for products that are manufactured in-house.
        Activating this route generally triggers manufacturing orders when the product's stock goes below the re-ordering rule threshold.

    Dropship:
        Products are sent directly from the supplier to the customer, bypassing the company's stock.
        Useful to avoid holding inventory for specific products.

    Replenish On Order (MTO - Make To Order):
        Products are procured or produced only when there is a confirmed customer order.
        Helps to avoid maintaining stock for items that are infrequently ordered or are customized per order.

    Push & Pull Routes:
        Push rules: Define operations that are triggered automatically when a move is processed. They dictate what needs to be done next.
        Pull rules: Determine what operation needs to be sourced. For example, when a location is running out of stock, a pull rule can trigger a route to resupply it.

    Cross-Dock:
        Products are received in an input zone and then directly shipped out from the output zone, minimizing storage in the internal location.

    Put Away Strategies:
        They define where products should be stored in the warehouse once they've been received. While not a route in the traditional sense, it's an integral part of product routing within the warehouse.

    Transship (if set up):
        Useful for multi-warehouse setups. When one warehouse is out of stock, it can procure the product from another warehouse within the same company.

Remember, the available routes can be based on the specific modules installed in your Odoo instance. For instance, if you don't have the Manufacturing module installed, you won't have manufacturing-related routes.

Routes can be set on the product and product category level, and they can be combined to achieve complex logistics scenarios. This flexibility is one of the reasons why Odoo's inventory management is powerful and can cater to various business needs.
